1. A cordless handheld vacuum lifter, comprising:

a rigid body comprising a handle;

a suction cup carried by the rigid body, the suction cup comprising a flexible sealing lip;

an electric vacuum pump disposed within the rigid body, the electric vacuum pump configured to draw air through a port so as to create a vacuum between the suction cup and a workpiece against which the flexible sealing lip is placed, a load being retained on the suction cup by the vacuum;

a pressure sensor configured to monitor the vacuum;

a warning indicator driven by the pressure sensor, the warning indicator configured to activate, as the vacuum is lost, at a warning vacuum which is reached before a release vacuum at which the suction cup ceases to retain the load, such that the warning indicator activates while the suction cup is still retaining the load; and

a rechargeable battery configured to power the electric vacuum pump.

2. The cordless handheld vacuum lifter of claim 1, wherein the warning indicator is configured to warn a user before the load can be dropped.

3. The cordless handheld vacuum lifter of claim 1, wherein the workpiece is a panel and the flexible sealing lip is placed against a face of the panel.

4. The cordless handheld vacuum lifter of claim 1, wherein the workpiece is a glass panel or a stone panel.

5. A method of lifting a workpiece with a cordless handheld vacuum lifter having a rigid body comprising a handle, a suction cup carried by the rigid body and comprising a flexible sealing lip, an electric vacuum pump disposed within the rigid body, a pressure sensor, a warning indicator, and a rechargeable battery, the method comprising:

placing the flexible sealing lip against a face of the workpiece;

drawing air through a port with the electric vacuum pump powered by the rechargeable battery, so as to create a vacuum between the suction cup and the workpiece;

carrying a load on the suction cup by the handle, the load being retained on the suction cup by the vacuum;

monitoring the vacuum with the pressure sensor while the load is retained; and

activating the warning indicator, driven by the pressure sensor, as the vacuum is lost, at a warning vacuum which is reached before a release vacuum at which the suction cup ceases to retain the load, such that the warning indicator is activated while the suction cup is still retaining the load.

6. The method of claim 5, wherein activating the warning indicator warns a user before the load can be dropped.

7. The method of claim 5, wherein the workpiece is a glass panel or a stone panel.

